---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.jsx";
import ProjectHero from "../../components/ProjectHero.jsx";
import ProjectOverview from "../../components/ProjectOverview.jsx";
import ResearchBlock from "@/components/ResearchBlock";
import {
  TypographyH3,
  TypographyH4,
  TypographyList,
  TypographyP,
} from "@/components/ui/Typography";
import ProductPageSection from "@/components/ProductPageSection";
import ImpactSection from "@/components/ImpactSection";
import ImageSection from "@/components/ImageSection";

import design_system from "@/assets/projects/DesignSystem/design_system_test.png";
import colorpalette from "@/assets/projects/DesignSystem/color_palette.png";
import spacingtokens from "@/assets/projects/DesignSystem/spacing_tokens.png";
import layoutgrid from "@/assets/projects/DesignSystem/layout_grid.png";
import typography from "@/assets/projects/DesignSystem/typography.png";
import components from "@/assets/projects/DesignSystem/components.png";
---

<Layout title="Design System">
  <ProjectHero
    title="Design System"
    subtitle="A scalable design system built for scalability and high-performance."
    year="2026"
    role="UX Designer & Frontend Developer"
    imageSrc={design_system.src}
    imageAlt="Project thumbnail"
    imageBgColor="bg-tertiary-500"
  />

  <ProjectOverview
    timeline="Always evolving"
    context="My original HTML/CSS portfolio grew repetitive and hard to scale, leading to visual inconsistencies. To fix this, I migrated to React, Next.js, and Tailwind CSS. Leveraging shadcn/ui for robust UI primitives, I customized the configuration to map my own comprehensive Design System tokens. This architecture allowed me to enforce strict visual consistency, eliminate duplicate code, and demonstrate my ability to bridge product design with scalable engineering."
    problem="Early iterations of the portfolio relied on a disjointed architecture that impacted development speed, visual clarity, and long-term maintainability. Specifically, I identified some issues commonly found in projects lacking a design system:"
    problems={[
      "Repetitive UI patterns leading to visual inconsistencies.",
      "Difficulties in scaling the codebase as the project grew.",
      "Manual maintenance of styles across multiple components, increasing the risk of bugs.",
      "Irregular spacing and alignment issues.",
      "Inconsistent typography and color usage.",
    ]}
    goals={[
      "Consistency: Create a unified visual language with clear guidelines.",
      "Scalability: Enable new pages and components to be built without rework.",
      "Efficiency: Reduce development time by providing reusable components.",
      "Accessibility: Ensure the design system is inclusive and usable for all users.",
      "Flexibility: Ensure the design system is flexible enough to accommodate future growth and changes.",
    ]}
    process="Research, Design tokens, components and tech implemetation"
    methodology="Atomic Design"
    tools={["Figma", "VS Code", "GitHub"]}
  />
  <!-- colocar a lista no problema -->

  <ResearchBlock
    title="Research"
    items={[
      {
        title: "Desk Research",
        description:
          "Before creating any components, I studied how established systems structure their foundations. I analyzed the design tokens, components, and documentation of Material Design, Ant Design, and Bootstrap to understand their approaches to scalability, consistency, and accessibility.",
      },
      {
        title: "Key Findings",
        description:
          "I discovered that successful design systems prioritize a clear visual language, reusable components, and comprehensive documentation. They also emphasize accessibility and responsiveness, ensuring that the system can adapt to various devices and user needs.",
      },
    ]}
  />
  <!-- aqui em cima no key findings vai uma lista -->

  <ProductPageSection
    title="Design System Foudations"
    description="Before building any components, I focused on establishing the foundations of the Design System. This involved defining core rules, structure, naming conventions, tokens, and controls. At the core of this foundation were design tokens, which act as the single source of truth for all design decisions."
  >
    <TypographyH3>Color Palette</TypographyH3>
    <TypographyP
      >A carefully curated selection of colors that align with the brand
      identity and ensure accessibility.</TypographyP
    >
  </ProductPageSection>
  <ImageSection
    src={colorpalette.src}
    alt="color palette image"
    maxWidth="max-w-[100%] md:max-w-[600px]"
  />

  <ProductPageSection>
    <TypographyH3>Typography</TypographyH3>
    <TypographyP
      >Hierarchy is expressed through weight, size, line height, and letter
      spacing, organized into five device-agnostic type roles:
    </TypographyP>
    <TypographyP
      >A typographic scale is essentially a set of text sizes that follow a
      consistent mathematical proportion, creating harmonious relationships
      between each level. In practice, when you define a typographic scale for
      your project, you establish a series of predetermined sizes that will be
      used consistently across the interface. Benefits include: visual
      consistency, clear hierarchy and efficiency in the design process.</TypographyP
    >
  </ProductPageSection>
  <ImageSection
    src={typography.src}
    alt="color palette image"
    maxWidth="max-w-[100%] md:max-w-[600px]"
  />

  <ProductPageSection>
    <TypographyH3>Spacing Tokens</TypographyH3>
    <TypographyP
      >The 4px base unit forms the foundation of our spacing token system,
      defined as spacing-xs. So everything becomes a multiple of 4. Every
      spacing token is derived as a multiple of this base unit, where the numeric
      suffix represents a percentage of the base value. it matches Tailwind,
      Chakra, Material systems. 4px is better than 8px.</TypographyP
    >
  </ProductPageSection>
  <ImageSection src={spacingtokens.src} alt="color palette image" />

  <ProductPageSection>
    <TypographyH3>Layout Grid</TypographyH3>
    <TypographyP
      >A grid is used to structure and position content in a way that creates
      consistent and balanced page layouts. It helps designers organize
      information clearly and maintain visual harmony across screens. A layout
      grid is made up of three key elements: columns, gutters, and margins.</TypographyP
    >
  </ProductPageSection>
  <ImageSection src={layoutgrid.src} alt="color palette image" />

  <ProductPageSection>
    <TypographyH3>Component Library</TypographyH3>
    <TypographyP
      >I implemented Atomic Design to establish a clear hierarchy and promote
      maximum reusability across my portfolio's interface. By breaking down UI
      elements into atoms, molecules, and organisms, I was able to build a
      scalable foundation that ensures visual consistency and simplifies the
      handoff from design to code.</TypographyP
    >
  </ProductPageSection>
  <ImageSection src={components.src} alt="atomic design image" />

  <ProductPageSection>
    <TypographyH3>Tech Implementation</TypographyH3>
    <TypographyP
      >For the portfolio's project gallery, I utilized the Shadcn Card component
      as the structural foundation. Following the Atomic Design methodology, I
      treated the individual Shadcn elements, such as CardHeader, CardTitle, and
      CardContent as atoms that were composed into a functional molecule (the
      Project Card).</TypographyP
    >
    <TypographyH4 className="mt-lg">Key technical decisions</TypographyH4>
    <TypographyList
      list={[
        "Composition: I nested atoms like Badge (for tech stacks) and Button (for links) within the Shadcn card structure to create a rich, informative organism.",
        "Using Tailwind CSS, I extended the base Shadcn styles to include custom hover effects and typography scales, ensuring the component matched my portfolio identity.",
        "Reusability: By defining a clear props structure for the image, title, and description, I transformed the static Shadcn template into a dynamic, reusable component that handles all project entries consistently.",
      ]}
    />
  </ProductPageSection>
  <!-- <ImageSection src={spacingtokens.src} alt="color palette image" /> -->

  <ImpactSection
    title="Impact"
    impacts={[
      {
        icon: "bolt",
        title: "Rework Reduction",
        description:
          "Shifted development from manual coding to component assembly, resulting in an over 40% reduction in development time when spinning up new pages and features.",
      },
      {
        icon: "dimensions",
        title: "Standardized Breakpoints & Optimization",
        description:
          "Built a fluid, modular layout system that perfectly bridges mobile and desktop viewports, driving 35% faster rendering and loading speeds by stripping away redundant CSS.",
      },
      {
        icon: "accessibility",
        title: "Acessibility",
        description: "Improved accessibility compliance across all components.",
      },
      {
        icon: "code",
        title: "Code Reusability",
        description:
          "Less duplicate code and visual inconsistencies across the portfolio and a more efficient development process.",
      },
    ]}
    nextSteps={[
      "Expand the token architecture to natively support multi-theme capabilities, starting with a fully semantic dark mode and fluid typography scales.",
      "Build living documentation using Storybook or Zeroheight to map out design tokens, component states, and clear usage guidelines for seamless scaling.",
    ]}
  >
    <TypographyList
      list={[
        "Tokens are the foundation of any system.",
        "A Design System is about decision‑making, scale, and efficiency.",
        "Even small projects benefit from systemic thinking.",
        "Collaboration between designers and developers is crucial for the success of a design system.",
      ]}
    />
    <TypographyP className="mt-lg">
      My core objective with this Design System was to architect the token
      layer, customize the naming conventions, and enforce systematic visual
      rules directly inside the Tailwind configuration. Instead of wasting
      velocity rewriting foundational UI components that the industry has
      already standardized, this project was a exercise in design governance,
      scalability, and semantic componentization to ensure design decisions
      structurally guided the final product.
    </TypographyP>
  </ImpactSection>
</Layout>
